## Turbofish call sites + alias-resolution chain (docs/16 §1 "Turbofish" +
## docs/07 "Type Aliases" + docs/11 "Borrowed string view").
##
## Surfaces exercised end-to-end:
##   - §1 Generic fun decl side: `fun identity<T>(arg: T) -> T` (one type-param,
##     one runtime param, identity return).
##   - §1 Turbofish at the call site: `identity<i32>(42)` rewrites through the
##     genExpr `.call` turbofish arm to `identity(i32, 42)` in zig (type-args
##     before runtime-args per zig's comptime-arg convention).
##   - §1 Turbofish carrying an alias type-arg: `identity<str>("hi")` rewrites
##     `str` to `[]const u8` via the `zagTypeToZig` alias-resolution helper
##     BEFORE the call expression emits, so zig sees
##     `identity([]const u8, "hi")` — the alias is transparent at the call
##     site exactly as it is at declaration sites (param annotations, struct
##     field types, union payload types, binding annotations, etc.).
##
## Why this lives in its own file:
##   The broader surface (§1-§4 generic-function shapes, §2 generic structs,
##   §5 generic impl blocks) is exercised by `examples/generics/generic_fun.zag`.
##   This file isolates the alias-resolution chain at the call site so a
##   regression in either half of the cycle-closure (commit 35412fb's parsePostfix
##   turbofish-precondition parser fix OR commit 6bc1c38's `zagTypeToZig` codegen
##   wrap) surfaces as a single-file failure under `examples/run_all.sh` —
##   cleanly pointing at the alias chain rather than entangling it with the
##   generic_fun.zag's order/traits/const surface.
##
## Running `examples/run_all.sh --check` (or `--build && run`) executes:
##     1. zag lexer + parser + codegen-emit on this file
##     2. zig build-exe on the emitted source
##   Any regression in the alias-resolution chain produces a step-2 zig
##   compile error (e.g. `unknown type name 'str'` if the wrap regresses, or
##   `expected gt, got '<'` if the parser precondition bug regresses).

# §1: single-type-param generic fun. Codegen emits
#     `pub fn identity(comptime T: type, arg: T) T { return arg; }`. The
#     `comptime T: type` slot is what lets zig treat T as a monomorphized
#     compile-time type parameter; the runtime `arg: T` is matched against
#     the call site's runtime arg. Without the bracket generic, every
#     call site would need a runtime `T` argument; turbofish at the call
#     site handles the dispatch by lifting the type-arg into the comptime
#     slot ahead of the runtime args.
fun identity<T>(arg: T) -> T {
    return arg;
}

fun main() {
    # Default-typed turbofish call site (no alias resolution needed —
    # i32 round-trips verbatim through `zagTypeToZig` because the helper
    # returns non-matching text unchanged). This call demonstrates that
    # the same plumbing path works for any type-arg, not just aliases.
    let int_val: i32 = 42;
    print("identity<i32>(int_val) = {identity<i32>(int_val)}\n");

    # Alias-typed turbofish call site. This is the surface the parser
    # precondition fix (commit 35412fb) unblocked + the codegen alias
    # wrap (commit 6bc1c38) keeps resolving. Pre-bind `s` so the template
    # interpolation is clean (no nested quote escapes inside `{...}`).
    let s: str = "hi";
    print("identity<str>(s) = {identity<str>(s)}\n");

    # Direct invocation without template-literal wrapping — exercises the
    # `.call` turbofish arm at expr_stmt position (no binding carve-out
    # applies, so the call result is consumed directly via print). Identical
    # alias-resolution path to the interpolated form above; demonstrates the
    # helper fires uniformly across expression contexts.
    print(identity<str>("hi"));
    print("\n");
}
